// The western West Indies: Jamaica, Cuba, Hispaniola, and the
// Bahamas.
//
// Coordinates are (latitude, longitude) in degrees, eyeballed from modern
// geography as a first pass. Refine against real coastline data later.
// Kingston Harbour is traced in detail; everything else is coarse.
//
// Historical notes:
//   - Port Royal sits on the western tip of the Palisadoes, a long sand spit
//     enclosing Kingston Harbour. The 1692 earthquake sank much of the town,
//     and a fire in 1703 burned most of the rest. In the game it stands as
//     the smaller town after the fire, the Royal Navy's base in the
//     Caribbean.
//   - Kingston, founded in 1692 on the harbour's north shore, has become
//     the island's commercial port: sugar, rum, and pimento out; provisions
//     and manufactures in.
//   - The Port Royal Cays lie off the point. Rackham's Cay is named for
//     "Calico Jack" Rackham, whose body was gibbeted there in 1720.
//   - Nassau, on New Providence, was sacked by the French and Spanish in
//     1703 and 1706 and left without a government. From 1716 it is the
//     "Republic of Pirates" of Hornigold, Jennings, and Blackbeard, until
//     Woodes Rogers arrives as governor in 1718.
//   - Santiago de Cuba, at the head of its deep pocket bay on Cuba's south
//     coast, is Spain's nearest port to Jamaica. It ships hides, salt beef,
//     and Cuban hardwood, and its guarda costas prey on English smugglers,
//     though the town buys Jamaican manufactures all the same.
//   - Tortuga, off Hispaniola, was the buccaneers' old haven in the 1600s:
//     Cayona on its south shore, where they cured meat and hides and
//     careened their ships. In the game it is a lawless anchorage that goes
//     to whoever looks after it.
//   - The Bahama Banks are drawn as impassable shoals. They are only a few
//     meters deep, and deep-water routes went around them.
//
// Port trade lists: `target` is the stock a market wants on hand and
// `per_day` is how fast it makes or uses that good. "Produces" covers both
// the port's own hinterland and what off-map shipping brings it. Sailors are
// men looking for a berth; soldiers are the garrison.
//
// A port's `tier` says how firmly it's held: a `Capital` is a nation's seat,
// a `Colony` a town like most (the default), and a `Haven` a lawless
// anchorage that changes hands often.
